Instead of building path costs directly, this checks whether a path is possible under a chosen limit. If every move on a route has a difference no larger than the limit, BFS can reach the destination. A larger limit can never make a reachable path impossible, so binary search fits the answer.

For example, with a limit of `2`, BFS only crosses edges whose height difference is at most `2`. If the target is reached, the search tries a smaller limit.

The grid is not modified. A one-cell grid immediately returns `0` because the start is already the destination.

```java
import java.util.ArrayDeque;
import java.util.Arrays;
import java.util.Deque;
import java.util.List;

class Solution {
    public int MinimumEffort(List<List<Integer>> heights) {
        int rows = heights.size();
        int cols = heights.get(0).size();

        int low = 0;
        int high = 1_000_000;

        while (low < high) {
            int limit = low + (high - low) / 2;
            if (canReach(heights, limit)) {
                high = limit;
            } else {
                low = limit + 1;
            }
        }

        return low;
    }

    private boolean canReach(List<List<Integer>> heights, int limit) {
        int rows = heights.size();
        int cols = heights.get(0).size();
        boolean[][] visited = new boolean[rows][cols];
        Deque<int[]> queue = new ArrayDeque<>();

        queue.offer(new int[]{0, 0});
        visited[0][0] = true;

        int[][] directions = {{1, 0}, {-1, 0}, {0, 1}, {0, -1}};

        while (!queue.isEmpty()) {
            int[] cell = queue.poll();
            int row = cell[0];
            int col = cell[1];

            if (row == rows - 1 && col == cols - 1) {
                return true;
            }

            for (int[] direction : directions) {
                int nextRow = row + direction[0];
                int nextCol = col + direction[1];

                if (nextRow < 0 || nextRow >= rows ||
                    nextCol < 0 || nextCol >= cols ||
                    visited[nextRow][nextCol]) {
                    continue;
                }

                int difference = Math.abs(
                    heights.get(row).get(col) - heights.get(nextRow).get(nextCol)
                );

                if (difference <= limit) {
                    visited[nextRow][nextCol] = true;
                    queue.offer(new int[]{nextRow, nextCol});
                }
            }
        }

        return false;
    }
}
```

Time complexity: `O(rows * cols * log(H))`, where `H` is the maximum possible height difference.

Space complexity: `O(rows * cols)` for the visited array and BFS queue.

This keeps the best known effort for every cell and repeatedly relaxes its neighbors. A cell goes into the queue again only when a smaller effort is found for it. The queue is not ordered like Dijkstra's heap, so a cell can be processed several times, but the code stays fairly simple.

The important update is still `max(currentEffort, difference)`. A path with a smaller maximum jump is better, even if it uses more cells.

```java
import java.util.ArrayDeque;
import java.util.Arrays;
import java.util.Deque;
import java.util.List;

class Solution {
    public int MinimumEffort(List<List<Integer>> heights) {
        int rows = heights.size();
        int cols = heights.get(0).size();
        int[][] best = new int[rows][cols];

        for (int[] row : best) {
            Arrays.fill(row, Integer.MAX_VALUE);
        }

        best[0][0] = 0;
        Deque<int[]> queue = new ArrayDeque<>();
        queue.offer(new int[]{0, 0});

        int[][] directions = {{1, 0}, {-1, 0}, {0, 1}, {0, -1}};

        while (!queue.isEmpty()) {
            int[] cell = queue.poll();
            int row = cell[0];
            int col = cell[1];
            int currentEffort = best[row][col];

            for (int[] direction : directions) {
                int nextRow = row + direction[0];
                int nextCol = col + direction[1];

                if (nextRow < 0 || nextRow >= rows ||
                    nextCol < 0 || nextCol >= cols) {
                    continue;
                }

                int difference = Math.abs(
                    heights.get(row).get(col) - heights.get(nextRow).get(nextCol)
                );
                int nextEffort = Math.max(currentEffort, difference);

                if (nextEffort < best[nextRow][nextCol]) {
                    best[nextRow][nextCol] = nextEffort;
                    queue.offer(new int[]{nextRow, nextCol});
                }
            }
        }

        return best[rows - 1][cols - 1];
    }
}
```

Time complexity: worst case `O((rows * cols)^2)` because cells may be placed in the queue many times.

Space complexity: `O(rows * cols)` for the best-effort table and queue.

A min heap works well here because the cost of a path is the largest height difference seen so far, not the sum of all differences. For each cell, `best[r][c]` stores the smallest effort found to reach it. When moving to a neighbor, the new effort is `max(currentEffort, heightDifference)`.

The input grid is only read, so it is not changed.

```cpp
#include <vector>
#include <queue>
#include <tuple>
#include <functional>
#include <climits>
#include <cstdlib>
#include <algorithm>
using namespace std;

class Solution
{
public:
    int MinimumEffort(vector<vector<int>> &heights)
    {
        int rows = heights.size();
        int cols = heights[0].size();

        vector<vector<int>> best(rows, vector<int>(cols, INT_MAX));
        priority_queue<tuple<int, int, int>,
                       vector<tuple<int, int, int>>,
                       greater<tuple<int, int, int>>> minHeap;

        best[0][0] = 0;
        minHeap.push({0, 0, 0});

        int directions[4][2] = {{1, 0}, {-1, 0}, {0, 1}, {0, -1}};

        while (!minHeap.empty())
        {
            auto [effort, row, col] = minHeap.top();
            minHeap.pop();

            if (effort != best[row][col])
            {
                continue;
            }

            if (row == rows - 1 && col == cols - 1)
            {
                return effort;
            }

            for (auto &direction : directions)
            {
                int nextRow = row + direction[0];
                int nextCol = col + direction[1];

                if (nextRow < 0 || nextRow >= rows ||
                    nextCol < 0 || nextCol >= cols)
                {
                    continue;
                }

                int step = abs(heights[row][col] - heights[nextRow][nextCol]);
                int nextEffort = max(effort, step);

                if (nextEffort < best[nextRow][nextCol])
                {
                    best[nextRow][nextCol] = nextEffort;
                    minHeap.push({nextEffort, nextRow, nextCol});
                }
            }
        }

        return 0;
    }
};
```

Time complexity: `O(rows * cols * log(rows * cols))`.

Space complexity: `O(rows * cols)` for the effort table and heap.

This uses the same monotonic idea as a threshold search, but the reachability check is recursive DFS. For a guessed limit, DFS ignores every neighbor whose height difference is too large. A successful check means the answer can be this limit or smaller.

The search range is based on the largest height in the grid. The largest possible difference is no more than `maxHeight - minHeight`, which is enough for the upper bound.

Dry run: if the first limit lets DFS reach the bottom-right cell, the upper half of the search is discarded. If DFS gets stuck, the limit is increased.

```python
class Solution:
    def MinimumEffort(self, heights):
        rows = len(heights)
        cols = len(heights[0])
        low = 0
        high = max(max(row) for row in heights) - min(min(row) for row in heights)

        directions = ((1, 0), (-1, 0), (0, 1), (0, -1))

        def reachable(limit):
            visited = [[False] * cols for _ in range(rows)]

            def dfs(row, col):
                if row == rows - 1 and col == cols - 1:
                    return True

                visited[row][col] = True

                for row_change, col_change in directions:
                    next_row = row + row_change
                    next_col = col + col_change

                    if not (0 <= next_row < rows and 0 <= next_col < cols):
                        continue
                    if visited[next_row][next_col]:
                        continue

                    difference = abs(
                        heights[row][col] - heights[next_row][next_col]
                    )
                    if difference <= limit and dfs(next_row, next_col):
                        return True

                return False

            return dfs(0, 0)

        while low < high:
            limit = (low + high) // 2
            if reachable(limit):
                high = limit
            else:
                low = limit + 1

        return low
```

Time complexity: `O(rows * cols * log(R))`, where `R` is the height range.

Space complexity: `O(rows * cols)` for the visited matrix and recursion stack.

For a single cell, `low` and `high` are both zero, so the method returns `0`. The input grid is only read.
